{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 18,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.087,
            0.888,
            0.123
          ],
          "content": "4. Place 1 coin in the left pan (call it effort) and 1 identical coin in the right pan (call it load). Observe that the beam stays horizontal."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.125,
            0.89,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "5. Add one more coin to the right pan, so that it contains 2 coins. The beam tilts. Move the heavier pan closer to the centre of the beam to balance the beam. **Measure** its distance from the centre."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.18,
            0.888,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": "6. Repeat step 5 with 4 coins and then 8 coins in the right pan. Each time, note its distance from the centre that balances the beam."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.217,
            0.888,
            0.253
          ],
          "content": "7. **Record** all observations and measurements, and complete the Table 7.1 by adding more rows."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.227,
            0.284,
            0.886,
            0.422
          ],
          "content": "| Number of Coinsin left pan,$n _ { 1 }$(Effort) | Distance of leftpan from thefulcrum,$L _ { 1 }$(cm) | Number of coinsin right pan,$n _ { 2 }$(Load) | Distance of rightpan from thefulcrum,$L _ { 2 }$(cm) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 |  | 1 |  |\n| 1 |  | 2 |  |",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.223,
                0.263,
                0.828,
                0.28
              ],
              "content": "Table 7.1: Number of coins in the left pan and its distance from the fulcrum"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_table_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.439,
            0.888,
            0.474
          ],
          "content": "By analysing the values recoded in Table 7.1, you can **conclude** that the beam balances when"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.482,
            0.474,
            0.625,
            0.493
          ],
          "content": "n_{1} \\times L_{1}^{*} = n_{2} \\times L_{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.494,
            0.259,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": "Or,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.376,
            0.494,
            0.887,
            0.512
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{effort} \\times \\mathrm{effort} \\; \\mathrm{arm} = \\mathrm{load} \\times \\mathrm{l} \\; \\mathrm{load} \\; \\mathrm{arm}\\tag{7.15}",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.514,
            0.888,
            0.567
          ],
          "content": "If the effort arm is increased, the effort required to move the same load is reduced. You can calculate the mechanical advantage of the lever by using Eq. (7.12). Thus,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.328,
            0.565,
            0.887,
            0.602
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{mechanism\\_advantage} = \\frac{\\mathrm{load}}{\\mathrm{effort}} = \\frac{\\mathrm{effort\\ arm}}{\\mathrm{load\\ arm}}\\tag{7.16}",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.602,
            0.889,
            0.67
          ],
          "content": "Hence, by increasing the effort arm, the lever applies a larger force $F _ { 2 }$ to the load than the effort $F_{1}^{\\cdot}$ The lever thus allows us to gain a mechanical advantage equal to the ratio of the distances, i.e., $\\frac { L _ { 1 } } { L _ { 2 } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.675,
            0.89,
            0.728
          ],
          "content": "With the use of a lever, the effort required is generally smaller but it has to move by a larger distance such that the total work done by the agency applying effort remains the same."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.082,
            0.741,
            0.305,
            0.894
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.126,
                0.898,
                0.265,
                0.913
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 7.34: A seesaw"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.318,
            0.73,
            0.89,
            0.798
          ],
          "content": "**Example 7.13:** For a seesaw having four seats A, B, D, E and fulcrum at C (Fig. 7.34), AC = EC = 2 m and BC = DC = 1 m. On which seats should children of masses 15 kg and 30 kg sit to make the seesaw balanced?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.318,
            0.801,
            0.89,
            0.851
          ],
          "content": "**Answer:** Suppose the child of mass 15 kg sits on seat A and the other child sits at a distance L from the fulcrum. Using Eq. (7.15), we obtain"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.35,
            0.855,
            0.555,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": "15\\; kg  \\times 2\\; m  = 30\\; kg  \\times L",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.35,
            0.876,
            0.423,
            0.893
          ],
          "content": "L = 1   \\mathrm { m }",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.32,
            0.896,
            0.684,
            0.915
          ],
          "content": "Thus, the other child should sit at seat D."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.075,
            0.943,
            0.115,
            0.96
          ],
          "content": "134"
        },
        {
